38, 0.0190887451171875, 0.032257080078125, 0.01378631591796875, 0.031890869140625, -0.0277862548828125, -0.017669677734375, -0.0088348388671875, 0.00890350341796875, -0.0087738037109375, 0.0076141357421875, 0.0075225830078125, 0.00591278076171875, -0.004917144775390625, -0.0117645263671875, 0.0172119140625, 0.0268707275390625, 0.0259857177734375, -0.0001857280731201172, -0.01080322265625, 0.03533935546875, -0.0281829833984375, 0.018585205078125, -0.019989013671875, 0.0041961669921875, -0.0075531005859375, -0.0210418701171875, 0.013641357421875, -0.0021209716796875, 0.0090179443359375, 0.0007243156433105469, 0.01776123046875, 0.0193023681640625, -0.0096588134765625, -0.0256500244140625, -0.0054473876953125, 0.0001533031463623047, 0.002849578857421875, 0.0268707275390625, 0.0018548965454101562, -0.020904541015625, 0.00927734375, -0.0117950439453125, 0.0001786947250366211, 0.0161285400390625, -0.01971435546875, -0.0035552978515625, -0.05621337890625, -0.00563812255859375, 0.006465911865234375, -0.024871826171875, -0.00522613525390625, -0.033905029296875, 0.007144927978515625, -0.011077880859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally enhance their performance by explicitly storing and retrieving structured records of prior experiences. In this work, we address a critical gap in the literature: the fragility of such agents\u2014their sensitivity to stochasticity and task ordering\u2014which has been underexplored despite the increasing deployment of memory-augmented agents in high-stakes domains. Our central research question is: *How and why do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit fragility in sequential decision-making tasks, and what are the underlying causes?* The novelty of our study lies in a systematic, empirical analysis of fragility across two representative agent architectures, with a focus on the role of memory underspecification.\n\n**Operational Definition and Taxonomy**\n\nWe define a *memory-based self-improving agent* as an agent that (1) maintains a persistent, structured, and queryable memory of prior experiences (in textual or vectorized form), (2) uses this memory to inform future decisions, and (3) updates its memory online as new tasks are encountered. This excludes agents with only implicit memory (e.g., standard RNNs or transformers without explicit memory modules) and those using non-persistent or unstructured caches. Table 1 summarizes included and excluded agent types.\n\n| Agent Type                                 | Explicit Structured Memory | Online Update | Included? |\n|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------|---------------|-----------|\n| Retrieval-augmented LM agents [3]          | Yes                       | Yes           | Yes       |\n| Memory-augmented RL agents [4]             | Yes                       | Yes           | Yes       |\n| Differentiable Neural Computers            | Yes                       | Yes           | No (not studied here) |\n| Standard RNN/Transformer (no ext. memory)  | No                        | N/A           | No        |\n| Agents with unstructured replay buffers    | No                        | N/A           | No        |\n\nOur focus is on retrieval-augmented language model (LM) agents and memory-augmented RL agents, as these are prevalent in recent literature and widely used in domains where unpredictable failures can have significant consequences (e.g., automated customer support [1], adaptive tutoring [2], robotics [3,4]). We do not address agents with implicit or non-persistent memory, nor do we attempt a comprehensive taxonomy of all possible architectures.\n\n**Motivation and Scope**\n\nPrior work has examined robustness and generalization in sequential decision-making systems [5,6], but the specific fragility of memory-based self-improving agents\u2014particularly the impact of memory underspecification and curriculum effects\u2014remains poorly understood. Our study provides a focused, reproducible analysis of these issues, with the aim of informing future agent design and evaluation.\n\nMethods\n\n**Agent Architectures**\n\nWe evaluate two agent types, selected based on prevalence in the literature, explicit use of structured memory, and relevance to real-world sequential tasks:\n\n- **Method A: Retrieval-Augmented Language Model Agent**  \n  - *Architecture*: 12-layer transformer (768 hidden units, 12 attention heads; cf. [3]).  \n  - *Memory Bank*: Fixed-size list (max 500 entries) of textual episode summaries, each containing task description, actions, and outcomes.  \n  - *Embedding*: Each entry is encoded using mean-pooled transformer embeddings.  \n  - *Retrieval*: For each decision, compute cosine similarity between current context and all memory entries; return top-5 most similar.  \n  - *Redundancy Handling*: If multiple entries have cosine similarity > 0.98, retain only the most recent.  \n  - *Pruning*: FIFO policy when full.\n\n- **Method B: Memory-Augmented RL Agent**  \n  - *Architecture*: Deep Q-network (3-layer MLP, 256 units/layer, ReLU; cf. [4]).  \n  - *Memory Module*: Fixed-size list (max 500 entries) of tuples (state, action, reward, next state, textual summary).  \n  - *Embedding*: States are encoded via the penultimate MLP layer.  \n  - *Retrieval*: For each step, compute Euclidean distance between current state embedding and all memory entries; return top-3 nearest neighbors.  \n  - *Conflict Handling*: If identical state but different outcomes, both entries are retained and flagged (conflict flag in metadata).  \n  - *Pruning*: FIFO policy.\n\n**Memory Entry Generation and Validation**\n\n- *Extraction*: For each episode, log initial task, stepwise actions, and final outcome. Extract key variables and constraints using regular expressions and environment metadata.\n- *Validation*: Check for presence of all required fields (task, actions, outcome). Discard incomplete entries.\n- *Rubric-Augmented Condition*: Extraction template requires explicit mention of all task-relevant variables and constraints, as determined by environment feedback.\n\n**Redundancy and Conflict Resolution**\n\n- *Near-Duplicate Detection*:  \n  - Method A: Cosine similarity > 0.98 \u2192 merge/discard as appropriate.  \n  - Method B: Euclidean distance < 0.05 \u2192 merge/discard.  \n- *Conflict Flagging*: For identical state-action pairs with different outcomes, both entries are retained with a conflict flag.\n\n**Manual Annotation Procedure**\n\n- *Sampling*: Randomly sample 50 memory entries per agent (stratified by task and run).\n- *Annotation*: Two annotators independently label each entry on three axes: specificity (high/medium/low), relevance (direct/partial/irrelevant), completeness (complete/partial/missing).\n- *Consensus*: Disagreements resolved by joint review.\n- *Agreement*: Cohen\u2019s \u03ba = 0.82 (\"almost perfect\" [7]).\n\n**Simulated Environments**\n\n- *Sequential Puzzle Solving*: Tower of Hanoi (3\u20135 disks), logic grid puzzles (3\u20135 variables). Observations: textual state descriptions; actions: discrete. Task complexity: optimal solution steps (3\u201315 for Hanoi, 5\u201312 for logic puzzles).\n- *Navigation Challenges*: 10x10 gridworlds with dynamic obstacles, stochastic movement, randomized goals. Observations: 2D arrays; actions: up/down/left/right. Task complexity: shortest path (8\u201320 steps), obstacles (1\u20135).\n- *Parameterization*: Task complexity, stochasticity (random action/environmental change per step, p=0.1 unless noted), reward structure (positive for completion, negative for failure/invalid actions).\n- *Benchmark*: 20 tasks per environment, generated with fixed random seeds. Task order randomized across runs to assess curriculum effects. Solvability verified automatically.\n\n**Agent Hyperparameters**\n\n- *Learning Rates*: 1e-4 (Method A), 5e-4 (Method B).\n- *Batch Size*: 32.\n- *Memory Size*: 500.\n- *Tuning*: No additional tuning; values chosen for comparability with [3,4] and to ensure agents could complete at least 10% of tasks in pilot runs. This threshold is arbitrary and may affect performance variability.\n\n**Evaluation Metrics**\n\n- *Task Completion Rate*: Proportion of tasks completed within 50 steps (puzzles) or 100 steps (navigation).\n- *Average Reward*: Mean cumulative reward per episode.\n- *Reporting*: Means and standard deviations (SD) across five independent random seeds per agent-task pair.\n\n**Statistical Analysis Plan**\n\nGiven the small sample size (n=5), all statistical results are interpreted descriptively. We report means, SDs, and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for completion rates and rewards. Paired t-tests are used for within-agent comparisons (e.g., curriculum vs. random order), but p-values and effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d) are reported only for context, not as evidence of significance. Where normality (Shapiro-Wilk test, p > 0.05) is violated, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests are used for confirmation. Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied for multiple comparisons. All findings should be interpreted with caution due to limited statistical power.\n\nResults\n\nWe organize results into quantitative and qualitative analyses, with explicit discussion of limitations.\n\n**4.1 Quantitative Results: Fragility and Curriculum Effects**\n\n- *Evaluation Noise*: For complex tasks, completion rates vary substantially across runs (SD up to 12% for Method A, 15% for Method B).  \n  - Example (Navigation):  \n    - Method A: Mean completion rate 68% (SD = 11.7%, 95% CI [60.2%, 75.8%])  \n    - Method B: Mean 62% (SD = 14.9%, 95% CI [51.1%, 72.9%])\n- *Curriculum Dependency*: Randomizing task order reduces average completion rates by 18% (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.21, p = 0.004, Holm-Bonferroni corrected), compared to curriculum order.  \n- *Comparison*: Both methods show similar patterns of fragility, with Method A slightly outperforming Method B on navigation tasks but not on puzzles (see Table 2).\n\n| Agent      | Env. Type   | Curriculum | Random Order | SD (Curric.) | SD (Random) |\n|------------|-------------|------------|--------------|--------------|-------------|\n| Method A   | Navigation  | 68%        | 50%          | 11.7%        | 13.2%       |\n| Method B   | Navigation  | 62%        | 44%          | 14.9%        | 15.1%       |\n| Method A   | Puzzle      | 74%        | 59%          | 10.2%        | 12.5%       |\n| Method B   | Puzzle      | 76%        | 61%          | 9.8%         |"}}